// ─── THE MERGED QUESTION BANK ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every question from the source material, deduplicated and tagged.
// `probe` entries carry DETECTS / ANSWER / FOLLOW-UP — these are the depth
// questions designed to separate people who did the work from people who read.
//
// USE THIS AS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A SCRIPT. For each question, try to answer
// BEFORE reading. Then bucket yourself:
//   1. Could answer from experience  → rehearse with your specifics attached
//   2. Understood it immediately     → practise saying it OUT LOUD
//   3. Needed the answer             → real gap: study it, or plan to be honest
// Bucket 3 is the useful output. It tells you where you're thin before an
// interviewer finds out.

export const BANK_ADVICE = {
  h: "Why generic answers score lower than specific ones",
  body: "Compare two answers to \"why did you choose that chunk size?\":\n\nA: \"400 tokens with overlap is a good default that balances context and precision.\"\n\nB: \"I started at 1000 because it seemed safe, and retrieval was poor — the embeddings were too diluted. I dropped to 400 with 15% overlap and recall@5 went from about 0.6 to 0.8 on my test queries. What actually fixed the remaining failures was parent-document retrieval, because line items only make sense with the header attached.\"\n\nA is correct. B gets the offer. Same knowledge, but B has a BEFORE, an AFTER, a NUMBER, and a SURPRISE. That's what \"has actually worked on this\" sounds like.\n\nWhere you have real experience, answer like B. Where you don't, don't fake B — a faked B collapses on the first follow-up.",
  warning: "These questions have second and third layers. The interviewer asks, you give a clean answer, and then they ask \"did you actually test both?\" A memorized answer has no second layer. It produces a confident first response followed by silence, which is a WORSE signal than not knowing at all — because now they know you were reciting.",
};

